From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jonathan Schwender <schwenderjonathan@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 17 Oct 2026 06:02:55 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] script: Suspend a media fetch instead of cancelling it when
 the player is full

When its player has enough data, a media element cancels a fetch with
range support, to restart it with a Range request once the player needs
data again, and keeps queueing the data of a fetch without range
support, up to 32 MiB, before it cancels it and fetches the resource
from its start again. Every restart costs a request, on HTTP/1.1 a new
connection, and the bytes that were in flight.

Ask net to suspend a fetch that is read from the network instead, and
queue the data that was in flight. Net answers whether it suspended the
fetch. If it did, flow control holds back the server, the download is
suspended (NETWORK_IDLE and suspend, then NETWORK_LOADING once it
resumes, and no progress events meanwhile), and once the player needs
data, the same fetch resumes and the queue drains. If it did not, as
for a cache hit or when two fetches to the origin are suspended
already, the fetch backs off as before; so it does if the answer is
lost, and it is resumed in case net suspended it. Responses that are not read
from the network are handled as before, and the cap on the queue stays
for data that keeps arriving. When a fetch completes, progress, then
NETWORK_IDLE and suspend follow, also if the fetch was suspended.

A fetch with range support that stays suspended for
media_fetch_suspension_timeout_ms (30 s) is cancelled, to restart where
the player stopped once it needs data again, so that an element paused
for long does not hold a connection, which servers would time out
anyway. A fetch without range support stays suspended, since its
restart would fetch the resource from its start again, until net gives
up on it after network_fetch_suspend_timeout_ms (60 s).

Such a server, and net, end the fetch: net with a network error, which
ran the fatal steps (MEDIA_ERR_NETWORK, "If the connection is
interrupted after some media data has been received"), and a closed
connection like a complete body, so the stream ended early. So if a
fetch that was suspended ends with a network error, or with fewer bytes
than its Content-Length, restart it at the byte where the player
stopped, with a Range request, which a server without range support
answers from the start of the resource, whose bytes before that
position are discarded as after a seek: at once if the player accepts
data, or else once it needs data again. A restart past the end of the
resource, which the server answers with 416 or which the length of the
resource tells, ends the stream, as when the server's Content-Length
was too large; so does a restart without range support whose body ends
without an error where the player stopped. Any other restart that ends
early before it delivers data runs the fatal steps: net ends a body
that loses its connection without an error. A restart that delivered
data may be restarted again, so a fetch is only restarted while it
makes progress. The Content-Length of an encoded body is not
the length of the decoded body, so it is not compared.

The state of the suspension in the fetch context grows HTMLVideoElement
from 1264 to 1296 bytes.

Fork note: meant for upstream. Drop once upstream has it.

Testing: no test; a fetch only backs off once the 50 MiB queue of the
GStreamer player is full. See Validation.

Validation: Linux, GStreamer, a 150 MB, 40 s H.264 file in a muted <video preload=metadata> that plays, from a Python server that sends at most 25 MB/s per connection; the base is the parent commit. With range support: 1 request and 149.7 MB sent (base: 3 requests, 149.9 MB); networkState turns NETWORK_IDLE with a suspend event once net confirms the suspension 3.5 s in, and NETWORK_LOADING when the fetch resumes 17.4 s in, with no seeking, stalled or error event. Without range support: 1 request, 149.7 MB, peak RSS 422 MiB (base: 2 requests, 269.9 MB, 419 MiB); paused for 25 s, 1 request, 149.7 MB (base: 2 requests, 269.9 MB). Paused for 40 s with range support: the timeout cancels the fetch 30 s after the suspension, and the restart when playback needs data again plays to the end: 2 requests, 155.4 MB (base: 3 requests, 149.9 MB). Three elements on one origin: net suspends two fetches, which take 1 request each, and the third backs off when net declines its suspension: 4 requests, 449.1 MB (base: 9 requests, 449.8 MB). A suspension costs peak RSS (423 instead of 392 MiB for one element, 738 instead of 683 MiB for three), although only 53 bytes were queued when net confirmed it: once the fetch resumes, net reads at once what the socket buffers held. A server that closes a connection it could not write to for 5 s: the earlier revision of this patch without the restart ended playback after 16.5 s of the media, with no error; with it, the media plays to the end, in 3 requests and 149.7 MB with range support and 2 requests and 238.3 MB without. With network_fetch_suspend_timeout_ms=5000 and playback paused for 40 s, net ends the suspended fetch with a network error, and the media plays to the end after the restart: 3 requests and 156.9 MB with range support, 2 requests and 239.1 MB without, no error event. A server whose Content-Length is 10 MB too large: playback ends normally, as on the base; at 25 MB/s the restart fetches the 10.5 MB that were queued again (2 requests, 160.2 MB) and, without range support, the whole resource again (2 requests, 299.3 MB); at 8 and 12 MB/s the restart starts at the end of the file, which the server answers with 416 (2 requests, 149.7 MB). A server that closes idle connections and closes the restart right after its headers: MEDIA_ERR_NETWORK at the restart, 16.5 s into the media, with and without range support. `./mach test-wpt --release --processes 6 --timeout-multiplier 2` html/semantics/embedded-content/media-elements, the-video-element and html/canvas/element/manual/drawing-images-to-the-canvas with the series: 451 tests, 446 as expected; the other five behave the same on the parent commit of the series when run alone: autoplay-default-permissions-policy.https.sub.html passes where a timeout is expected (3/3 on both), media_fragment_seek.html fails a subtest (3/3 on both), src_object_blob.html times out 1 of 3 runs on both, video_timeupdate_on_seek.html passes a subtest that is expected to time out, and load-removes-queued-error-event.html hangs intermittently because GStreamer never reports the metadata of the 539-byte sound_0.mp3 (3 of 8 runs with the series, 4 of 8 without, alternating). In a later run of media-elements, track-cues-seeking.html timed out, which it does intermittently with and without the series (3 of 39 and 2 of 39 isolated runs, alternating); its 192 KB video never fills the player. `./mach check --ohos` passes at each commit; `./mach test-tidy` reports only the known rustls advisories.
AI-assisted: Yes (agent session; implemented, tested and measured by the agent)
Signed-off-by: Jonathan Schwender <schwenderjonathan@gmail.com>
